258
00:23:24,820 --> 00:23:27,340
I think everybody on the council knows about the work of the National

259
00:23:27,340 --> 00:23:28,900
Children's Advocacy Center.

260
00:23:28,900 --> 00:23:30,780
We're founded on two basic concepts.

261
00:23:30,780 --> 00:23:34,220
Number one, the only way we can be successful with addressing child sexual

262
00:23:34,220 --> 00:23:38,500
abuse is by having a multidisciplinary team that includes law enforcement, child

263
00:23:38,500 --> 00:23:41,900
protective services, mental health professionals, medical professionals.

264
00:23:41,900 --> 00:23:44,780
And number two, whatever that team does, it should actually help kids.

265
00:23:44,780 --> 00:23:47,880
And Bud Kramer founded this concept back in the 80s.

266
00:23:47,880 --> 00:23:50,220
This model has grown.

267
00:23:50,220 --> 00:23:53,380
We're very appreciative of having our wonderful campus at the corner of Pratt

268
00:23:53,380 --> 00:23:56,260
and Meridian where you can see all of our partners that are co-located with us.

269
00:23:56,260 --> 00:23:59,980
A special note obviously to this council is our integral role that we play with

270
00:23:59,980 --> 00:24:02,100
Huntsville Police Department Special Victims Unit.

271
00:24:02,100 --> 00:24:07,180
That entire Special Victims Unit is co-located on our campus and we work together every day.

272
00:24:07,180 --> 00:24:12,760
We did two forensic interviews today for HPD on cases that they were investigating.

273
00:24:12,760 --> 00:24:17,620
We also provide a tremendous amount of training for those officers when they come to the unit

274
00:24:17,620 --> 00:24:19,460
and we have a great working relationship.

275
00:24:19,460 --> 00:24:23,980
You all do have a wonderful Special Victims Unit of investigators.

276
00:24:23,980 --> 00:24:24,980
So let me say that.

277
00:24:24,980 --> 00:24:31,380
I also would be remiss for me to not mention that this model that started in Huntsville,

278
00:24:31,380 --> 00:24:34,300
Alabama has now expanded all over the U.S.

279
00:24:34,300 --> 00:24:37,340
There's more than a thousand children's advocacy centers across the United States.

280
00:24:37,340 --> 00:24:41,580
So once again, Huntsville is showing entrepreneurial spirit that has literally revolutionized how

281
00:24:41,580 --> 00:24:45,860
our nation responds to child abuse and these programs are also operating in more than 40

282
00:24:45,860 --> 00:24:47,500
countries around the world.

283
00:24:47,500 --> 00:24:49,860
But I don't want to talk to you about child abuse necessarily.

284
00:24:49,860 --> 00:24:54,100
I want to talk to you about, because everybody thinks, oh, you address child abuse, but I

285
00:24:54,100 --> 00:24:59,420
want to talk to you about the impact of our work in the long term on human rights, on

286
00:24:59,420 --> 00:25:04,540
school performance and behavior, on economic development, on public health, on workforce

287
00:25:04,540 --> 00:25:09,580
development and on government efficiency, all in less than 4 minutes.

288
00:25:09,580 --> 00:25:12,820
So human rights, first, let me just ask the question, do kids have the right to grow up

289
00:25:12,820 --> 00:25:14,300
free from abuse or neglect?

290
00:25:14,300 --> 00:25:17,780
I believe every sane person would say yes.

291
00:25:17,780 --> 00:25:21,660
And secondly, if a child is abused, do they have the right to receive quality services?

292
00:25:21,660 --> 00:25:22,660
Yes.

293
00:25:22,660 --> 00:25:26,220
And literally, in this community, unfortunately, children's human rights have been violated

294
00:25:26,220 --> 00:25:30,580
whenever they're abused, but we as a system must make sure that we're not re-traumatizing

295
00:25:30,580 --> 00:25:31,580
them.

296
00:25:31,580 --> 00:25:34,180
And so we are addressing human rights.

297
00:25:34,180 --> 00:25:38,500
Individuals who have experienced child maltreatment are at increased risk of educational underachievement,

298
00:25:38,500 --> 00:25:43,780
both math and verbal scores, and they're more likely to have poor social skills and classroom

299
00:25:43,780 --> 00:25:44,780
behavior.

300
00:25:44,780 --> 00:25:49,460
So if we can intervene on behalf of children when they have had these incidents and prevent

301
00:25:49,460 --> 00:25:51,800
them from having the long term impacts, great.

302
00:25:51,800 --> 00:25:55,300
They're also less likely, individuals who have experienced abuse are less likely to

303
00:25:55,300 --> 00:25:59,580
have savings accounts, less likely to own their own car, less likely to have any kind

304
00:25:59,580 --> 00:26:02,980
of stocks or savings, and less likely to own their own home.

305
00:26:02,980 --> 00:26:07,940
And men who were sexually abused in childhood on average earn 21 percent less than their

306
00:26:07,940 --> 00:26:11,760
peers who were not abused, and for women it's 15 percent.

307
00:26:11,760 --> 00:26:14,980
This is data from individuals who did not receive services.

308
00:26:14,980 --> 00:26:18,980
We're trying to change that so that we increase the economic viability of this community by

309
00:26:18,980 --> 00:26:20,740
addressing trauma in childhood.

310
00:26:20,740 --> 00:26:25,260
Additionally, on public health impacts, the adverse childhood research is a big study,

311
00:26:25,260 --> 00:26:30,180
but across mental health issues and chronic disease, the more adverse childhood experiences

312
00:26:30,180 --> 00:26:33,400
we have without intervention, we're going to have more issues.

313
00:26:33,400 --> 00:26:34,580
It also impacts cost.

314
00:26:34,580 --> 00:26:38,700
Nine percent of all Medicaid costs are associated with child maltreatment.

315
00:26:38,700 --> 00:26:42,280
And the cost of health care goes up when we have more health care issues.

316
00:26:42,280 --> 00:26:45,160
For women who were sexually abused, it's 16 percent higher.

317
00:26:45,160 --> 00:26:48,940
For women who were both physically and sexually abused, it's 36 percent higher.

318
00:26:48,940 --> 00:26:52,060
So these are numbers that impact the future of this community's health.

319
00:26:52,060 --> 00:26:53,900
It's also an issue with workforce development.

320
00:26:53,900 --> 00:26:57,900
The more adverse childhood experiences an individual had, they're more likely to have

321
00:26:57,900 --> 00:27:01,580
absenteeism on the job, financial problems, and job problems.

322
00:27:01,580 --> 00:27:05,960
So we need to address this in childhood so we have a better workforce moving forward.

323
00:27:05,960 --> 00:27:11,220
And finally, again, on government efficiency, we save $1,000 by reducing the duplication

324
00:27:11,220 --> 00:27:14,100
of government activities and having this coordinated approach.

325
00:27:14,100 --> 00:27:18,500
So it's a smart investment for all of these reasons, and I'm under 4 minutes.

